<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements for the signed app.

  Every entry is here because something breaks without it, and nothing is here
  "just in case" — each one widens what a compromised Mull could do:

    allow-jit / unsigned-executable-memory
      Chromium's JavaScript engines. Non-negotiable for Electron.

    disable-library-validation
      Mull loads two things Apple didn't sign: the better-sqlite3 native module
      and the `mull-mac` sidecar. Both are signed with the same Team ID, but the
      loader still refuses them under library validation.

    device.audio-input
      The microphone. Paired with NSMicrophoneUsageDescription in the app's
      Info.plist (see electron-builder.yml) — without the string, macOS kills
      the process rather than prompting.

    automation.apple-events
      Browser tabs, and nothing else. This entry is new, and it replaces a note
      here that said the opposite — "Mull drives apps through CGEvent and the
      Accessibility API, never by scripting them" — so it is worth saying what
      changed and why, because this is the widest entry in the file.

      A browser tab is not a window. It never appears in kAXWindowsAttribute,
      it carries no URL, and Chromium exposes only the frontmost tab's content
      with no identity attached. So through the Accessibility API alone Mull
      could not say which page it was looking at, could not tell two tabs apart
      and could not go back to one. AppleScript asks the browser instead, and it
      is the only route that needs no extension, no debugging port and no page
      access. See src/main/services/browser.ts.

      What this widens: with this entitlement Mull *may* send Apple Events to
      any application the user consents to. Consent is still per-application and
      still the user's — macOS prompts on the first event to each target, and
      until they answer, the call fails and Mull carries on without it. Automation
      is granted per target, so a user may allow Chrome and refuse System Events;
      each failure degrades on its own and says which one it was.

      What Mull actually sends is seven fixed scripts: three against a table of
      nine browser bundle ids, asking what tabs are open, and one against System
      Events, asking which applications are running. Every argument is passed as
      an argument rather than spliced into the script, and `npm run
      check:applescript` compiles all seven without running any of them.

      Paired with NSAppleEventsUsageDescription in electron-builder.yml, under
      the same rule as the microphone: without the string macOS kills the
      process instead of prompting.

  Deliberately absent: app sandbox — Accessibility control of other apps is
  impossible inside it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
